1
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:09,874
Did you know that in futures, you can take an opportunity when prices rise, and also when prices fall?

2
00:00:10,614 --> 00:00:14,860
Let us see how that works, and why both directions still carry risk.

3
00:00:16,760 --> 00:00:23,646
In this video, we explain long and short positions using FCPO, and how profit and loss are calculated.

4
00:00:26,346 --> 00:00:32,536
A long position means you buy first, expecting the price to rise, and sell later to close the position.

5
00:00:32,876 --> 00:00:44,370
Example: FCPO is at RM4,000 per metric tonne. You buy one contract. The price rises to RM4,020, and you sell to close.

6
00:00:44,710 --> 00:00:55,076
One FCPO contract represents 25 metric tonnes. So the RM20 move is RM20 times 25, or RM500 gross profit.

7
00:00:57,176 --> 00:01:03,702
A short position is the reverse: you sell first, expecting the price to fall, and buy later to close.

8
00:01:04,042 --> 00:01:11,504
You do not need to own any palm oil to sell first. You are entering a contract obligation, which you close by buying it back.

9
00:01:11,844 --> 00:01:24,634
Sell one contract at RM4,000. The price falls to RM3,980, and you buy back. Again, RM20 times 25 is RM500 gross profit.

10
00:01:26,734 --> 00:01:28,556
Here they are side by side.

11
00:01:28,896 --> 00:01:35,038
Long is bullish: buy first, sell later. Short is bearish: sell first, buy later.

12
00:01:35,378 --> 00:01:43,272
This is possible because every futures trade is an agreement between a buyer and a seller. Going short is as standard as going long.

13
00:01:45,372 --> 00:01:47,194
Now the other side of the coin.

14
00:01:47,534 --> 00:01:57,972
If you are long at RM4,000 and the price falls to RM3,980, you lose RM20 times 25, or RM500.

15
00:01:58,312 --> 00:02:06,830
If you are short at RM4,000 and the price rises to RM4,020, you also lose RM500.

16
00:02:07,170 --> 00:02:12,544
The same RM20 move that creates a profit for one position creates a loss for the other.

17
00:02:14,644 --> 00:02:16,466
So here is the simple formula.

18
00:02:16,806 --> 00:02:23,836
For a long position: exit price minus entry price, times 25, times the number of contracts.

19
00:02:24,176 --> 00:02:31,110
For a short position: entry price minus exit price, times 25, times the number of contracts.

20
00:02:31,450 --> 00:02:36,176
These are gross figures, before brokerage, exchange and clearing fees.

21
00:02:38,276 --> 00:02:41,658
Two more terms: opening and closing a position.

22
00:02:41,998 --> 00:02:47,396
Opening means entering the trade: you buy to open a long, or sell to open a short.

23
00:02:47,736 --> 00:02:54,622
Closing means taking the opposite trade in the same contract month: sell to close a long, buy to close a short.

24
00:02:54,962 --> 00:03:00,576
Until it is closed, the position stays open, and its value changes with every price move.

25
00:03:02,676 --> 00:03:08,818
One last, very important point: getting the direction right does not guarantee a profitable trade.

26
00:03:09,158 --> 00:03:20,508
Here, the price rises overall from RM4,000 to RM4,030. But a trader buys late, at RM4,035, near the high.

27
00:03:20,848 --> 00:03:33,182
The price pulls back, and the trader exits at RM4,012. That is minus RM23 times 25, a loss of RM575, even though the market went up.

28
00:03:33,522 --> 00:03:39,592
Entry and exit prices, execution, and transaction costs all affect the final result.

29
00:03:41,692 --> 00:03:45,098
Long means buy first, expecting prices to rise.

30
00:03:45,438 --> 00:03:48,820
Short means sell first, expecting prices to fall.

31
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:54,990
For FCPO, every RM1 per tonne move is worth RM25 per contract.

32
00:03:55,330 --> 00:03:59,120
The same move that profits one side causes a loss for the other.

33
00:03:59,460 --> 00:04:03,058
A position stays open until you close it with the opposite trade.

34
00:04:03,398 --> 00:04:06,492
The right direction alone does not guarantee a profit.

35
00:04:07,792 --> 00:04:15,062
In futures, opportunities exist when prices rise or fall. But every position still carries the risk of loss.

36
00:04:17,162 --> 00:04:20,928
This is only the foundation for understanding the futures market.

37
00:04:21,268 --> 00:04:24,266
Know the product you trade before taking any risk.

38
00:04:24,606 --> 00:04:27,676
Continue your learning with Capital Market Institute.

39
00:04:29,576 --> 00:04:35,238
The references for this video are shown on screen. Always check the latest official specifications.

40
00:04:38,138 --> 00:04:47,016
For educational purposes only. This is not financial advice, and it is not a guarantee of profit. Futures trading involves a high risk of loss.
